# Overhaul token handling and playbook reliability

## Why

Token caching was non-deterministic: sometimes connecting took two browser
sign-ins and then everything was silent, other times it took three (Graph,
Exchange, and IPPS) and every new PowerShell session demanded a full re-auth.
Playbook runs were similarly unreliable, with most steps silently failing on
some runs. The root causes turned out to be layered: access tokens were cached
in globals on top of MSAL's own cache, account selection picked an arbitrary
cached account (a problem once the cache holds one admin account per customer
tenant), the persistent disk cache depended on a runtime NuGet download that
could silently fail, and playbook workers received a static token snapshot
plus whatever module version happened to be installed.

## What changed

**Token handling.** A single token authority, `Get-IRTAccessToken`, now mints
access tokens on demand from the MSAL cache. Cached accounts are tried in
tenant-aware order (last-known-good, then accounts homed in the target tenant,
then guests) before ever prompting. Raw token strings no longer live in
`$Global:IRT_Session` - only the expiry of the token bound into each SDK
context is tracked. Exchange and IPPS share one MSAL app, which makes IPPS
sign-in always silent after an Exchange sign-in. The MSAL cache-extension DLL
is bundled with the module (hash-pinned restore in PreBuild) instead of being
downloaded from nuget.org mid-connect, and cache registration failures are
loud errors instead of buried warnings.

The result: first contact with a tenant costs two browser sign-ins (the second
is an SSO click-through), and with `EnableTokenCache` on, every subsequent
session, parallel session, and playbook runspace connects silently for the
refresh token's lifetime.

**Refresh behavior.** `Update-IRTToken` refreshes only the service whose bound
token is stale, and Exchange/IPPS reconnects are scoped by ConnectionId so
refreshing one never tears down the other. `Connect-IRT -Refresh` no longer
drops scopes added via `-AdditionalScope`, and granting tenant-wide admin
consent now force-refreshes and re-binds the Graph token instead of running on
the pre-consent token for up to an hour.

**Playbook.** Workers mint their own Exchange tokens silently from the shared
MSAL cache per step (`Connect-IRTRunspaceExchange`), so long playbooks no
longer die when the snapshot token expires. Workers import the parent's module
version by path instead of whatever is installed (the cause of the mass step
failures), `Import-Module` calls are serialized with a mutex (fixing the
intermittent "Collection was modified" step errors), worker module loads no
longer reset the parent terminal's title, and the dependency scan now runs
once per session with the result shared into workers through a portable,
module-agnostic table. `Get-IRTLicenseReport` drops the unreliable external
Write-PSObject dependency.

**Tooling.** `Tests.ps1` and `Find-ModuleRoot` now resolve the module name
from the manifest instead of the checkout folder name, so the documented test
flow works inside git worktrees.

## Compatibility

No exported command changed its signature, and existing token caches keep
working (same format, path, and client IDs). The one incompatible surface is
the internal `$Global:IRT_Session` shape: `.Token`, `.TokenExpiry`, and
per-service `.PublicClientApplication` are gone. Scripts that read those
should call `Get-IRTAccessToken` instead.

## Testing

- Offline Pester: 332 passed, 0 failed. All-new branch coverage for the token
  authority, account ordering, PCA pooling, the three connectors (aud
  validation, reconnect matrix, scoped disconnects), Update-IRTToken, and the
  runspace helper.
- Online Pester against the test tenant: 44 passed, 0 failed, including a new
  end-to-end playbook test (zero step errors, all expected spreadsheets,
  parent connection and terminal title intact) and a live runspace-pool test.
  The cache round-trip (interactive sign-in, session cleared, silent
  reconnect) is exercised by the harness on every online run.
- Built-module verification: `Build.ps1` then `Tests.ps1 Offline -Built`
  green, proving the bundled DLL resolves from the built layout.
- Formatting battery and PSSA: clean.
